Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/1] selftests/bpf: bpf_flow: handle PPPoE session packets
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

This single-patch RFC adds PPPoE session-packet handling to the
in-tree BPF flow_dissector reference at
tools/testing/selftests/bpf/progs/bpf_flow.c.

Background — same XDP2 comparison work as my other RFC
=======================================================

This is a follow-up to my first kernel submission
([PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: 5-tuple-only hash
helper + sch_cake adoption), sent separately to netdev.
Both findings came out of the same XDP2-vs-kernel
comparison work done with Tom Herbert and team on the
XDP2 project (https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2).
This BPF patch is separated from the flow_dissector
series because it touches a different file, different
maintainer audience, and different test surface — but
mentioning the shared origin so reviewers see the same
analytical work behind both.

The issue
=========

bpf_flow.c's parse_eth_proto() dispatches on outer
ETHERTYPE to per-protocol BPF tail-call sub-programs.
The current switch handles:

  ETH_P_IP, ETH_P_IPV6, ETH_P_MPLS_MC, ETH_P_MPLS_UC,
  ETH_P_8021Q, ETH_P_8021AD

ETH_P_PPP_SES (0x8864 — PPPoE session packets) is missing.
PPPoE packets hit the default arm and are dropped without
parsing.

The kernel's C flow dissector handles PPPoE properly —
see the ETH_P_PPP_SES arm in __skb_flow_dissect()
(net/core/flow_dissector.c) which validates the PPPoE
session header via is_pppoe_ses_hdr_valid(), advances
past PPPOE_SES_HLEN (8 bytes, including the PPP protocol
field), and dispatches to the inner ethertype.

Any setup that uses the BPF flow_dissector as an
alternative to the C one (BPF_PROG_TYPE_FLOW_DISSECTOR)
loses PPPoE coverage. Per the BPF flow_dissector
selftests this is the reference program loaded by
flow_dissector_load.c.

Found while running the XDP2 protocol-coverage matrix
against the BPF reference dissector — the discrepancy
surfaces immediately on any pcap with PPPoE-wrapped
flows. A more detailed write-up of the discovery lives
in
perf-results/2026-05-20-bpf-pppoe-investigation/findings.md
on the same XDP2 branch as the flow_dissector RFC.

The patch
=========

PROG(PPPOE) follows the same pattern as the existing
PROG(VLAN):

- Local pppoe_ses_hdr struct (6-byte PPPoE session
  header per RFC 2516 + 2-byte PPP protocol field;
  matches PPPOE_SES_HLEN in <linux/if_pppox.h>).
- Validate ver/type (vertype == 0x11) and code (== 0)
  exactly as is_pppoe_ses_hdr_valid() does in the C path.
- Switch on PPP protocol:
    PPP_IP (0x0021)   -> tail-call IP
    PPP_IPV6 (0x0057) -> tail-call IPV6
  Anything else (LCP / PAP / CHAP / IPCP / etc) returns
  BPF_DROP — not a data flow.
- Advance keys->nhoff and keys->thoff by PPPOE_SES_HLEN.
- Update keys->n_proto for the inner protocol.

MAX_PROG bumped from 6 to 7; PPPOE = 6 added to the
identifier list. The userspace loader at
tools/testing/selftests/bpf/flow_dissector_load.h
iterates programs in source order and populates jmp_table
indices automatically, so no loader change is needed.

What this is NOT
================

- NOT a new bpf_flow_keys ABI field. session_id stays
  unexposed in BPF; the C dissector surfaces it via
  FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_PPPOE but bpf_flow_keys has no
  corresponding slot. Adding one is a separate
  UAPI conversation.
- NOT MPLS-over-PPP support. The C dissector dispatches
  PPP_MPLS_UC / PPP_MPLS_MC to the MPLS handler; this
  patch only does IP and IPv6 to keep the diff minimal.
  Easy follow-up if requested.
- NOT a refactor of bpf_flow.c. Just the smallest change
  that closes the documented gap.

Pre-post checklist
==================

- checkpatch.pl --strict passes (0 errors, 0 warnings,
  0 checks).
- bpf_flow.bpf.o compiles clean (clang 18.1.8 -target bpf,
  with nix hardening flags disabled — see the libelf /
  bpf-target workaround documented in the companion
  flow_dissector series).
- Verified the resulting BPF object exposes
  flow_dissector_6 (the new PPPOE program) alongside the
  existing flow_dissector_{0..5}.
- Still TODO: run the existing BPF flow_dissector
  selftests (tools/testing/selftests/bpf/test_progs
  -t flow_dissector); add a PPPoE pcap test case (the
  current pcap fixtures in
  tools/testing/selftests/bpf/ don't include PPPoE).

This is my first BPF subsystem patch; sent as RFC to
get feedback on the test-case strategy and on whether to
expose session_id via a UAPI extension as a follow-up
before formal submission.

Thanks,
Dave

